<section aria-labelledby="word-comments">
<h2 id="word-comments">Keep editorial comments as private feedback</h2>
<p>After previewing a Word document, choose <strong>Recover Word comment text</strong>. This separate action reads the standard <code>word/comments.xml</code> part locally. Open <strong>Inspect recovered comment text</strong> to read the feedback. The article preview and current writing stay unchanged. Author names, dates, comment anchors, thread relationships, resolved status, formatting, and hyperlink targets are not reproduced. File IDs are not the comment numbers shown by Word.</p>
<p>The recovery lists all supported comment text in file order, including unanchored comments and possible replies. It does not filter by Word's visibility settings: text hidden by styling can be included. Check the original document before keeping or sharing feedback. Select the private-report acknowledgement to download an unencrypted text file. That report contains comments, not the article or workspace source notes.</p>
<p>For article replacement, you can explicitly include the recovered comments as private review notes. The existing workspace limit is twelve notes of up to 500 characters each, including their comment-ID labels. Longer or more numerous comments remain available in the separate report; nothing is truncated. Including comments clears the import acknowledgement so you can review again. Article insertion keeps your existing review notes and does not add imported comments.</p>
<p>After replacement, included comments appear under Source notes &amp; review, in Review questions &amp; notes. Keep them with an explicit private workspace backup. They are excluded from article Markdown, Word, WordPress, HTML, and publishing kits. Applying or clearing the import discards the temporary recovery and its prepared report. Download before applying if you need the separate file.</p>
<p>Supports up to 100 comments, one to ten ordinary paragraphs per comment, up to 4,000 characters per comment and 100,000 characters overall. Paragraph breaks, tabs, line breaks, and ordinary run text are retained; formatting is flattened. The comment XML has a 2 MiB part limit and selected XML has a 4 MiB total limit. Fields, tracked changes, drawings, tables, unsupported wrappers, invalid relationships, and damaged files stop comment recovery without invalidating the article preview. The original Word document remains the reference for thread context and unsupported annotations.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="word-notes">
<h2 id="word-notes">Keep referenced Word notes</h2>
<p>Ordinary footnotes and endnotes become visible markers such as <code>[Word footnote 1]</code> and <code>[Word endnote 1]</code>, with matching labeled sections appended to the article. Repeated references to one note reuse its label. Safe web links, supported emphasis, and paragraph breaks inside the note are retained. Footnotes and endnotes are numbered separately by first visible reference.</p>
<p>These notes become article text and are included in article exports. Original Word numbering, superscripts, page-bottom placement, native footnote objects, and clickable note links are not reproduced. Compare every marker and note with the original before acknowledging the conversion. After combining articles or editing their labels, recheck the references yourself. Unreferenced notes and separator content are excluded; private workspace source notes and review questions remain separate.</p>
<p>Supports up to 100 referenced notes and 1,000 visible references, with at most 200 ordinary definitions in the file. Each referenced note must contain one to ten ordinary paragraphs and at most 4,000 converted characters. The article and all appended notes must fit the shared 60,000-character limit. Custom reference marks, nested note references, fields, tracked changes, headings, lists, tables, images, and other complex content inside a referenced note need simplification in Word first. A missing, ambiguous, malformed, or oversized note blocks import before replacing writing; nothing is silently truncated.</p>
</section>
